---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import PageIntro from "../components/PageIntro.astro";
import { href, source } from "../lib/links";
---

<Layout
  title="Research"
  description="Research question, methods and boundaries for network anomaly detection and traceability."
  section="Research"
>
  <PageIntro
    eyebrow="01 / Research"
    current="Research"
    title="Research with a clear boundary."
    description="The project investigates network-flow detection, cross-dataset validity and traceable decision evidence. All four models now have Held-out Test evaluations on main alongside a scoped Tauri 2 + FastAPI desktop prototype."
  />
  <div class="container section research-grid">
    <article class="prose">
      <h2 id="problem">The problem</h2><p>
        Labelled intrusion benchmarks make model comparison possible, but a high
        score on one dataset can reflect repeated flows or laboratory-specific
        patterns. A useful detection system also needs a traceable path from an
        observation to the evidence, without treating an IP address as proof of
        a person's identity.
      </p>
      <h2 id="objectives">Research objectives</h2><ol>
        <li>
          Establish a reproducible baseline on verified NF-UNSW-NB15-v3 bytes.
        </li><li>
          Compare supervised classification (Random Forest, XGBoost) with
          unsupervised anomaly scores (Isolation Forest, Autoencoder) under
          explicit split, benign-only training and threshold rules.
        </li><li>
          Audit shortcuts, equivalent feature vectors, false positives, false
          negatives and cross-dataset degradation (`NF-CSE-CIC-IDS2018-v3`).
        </li><li>
          Deliver a fail-closed inference pipeline with OOD abstention,
          deterministic priority scoring, NetFlow evidence timelines, dry-run
          response simulation and a local desktop prototype (`v0.6.0`).
        </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="method">Method</h2><p>
        The published v3 CSV is verified by manifest and quality checks. A
        chronological, group-aware split with 5-minute purge gaps assigns Train,
        Validation and Test before learned preprocessing. The baseline model
        contract keeps 47 ordered flow features and separates six trace metadata
        fields and the two targets. Train-only statistics are frozen for later
        partitions. Validation selects thresholds (`max-F1-on-known-attacks` for
        Isolation Forest; `fpr_constrained` at `FPR <= 0.05` for Autoencoder
        with `Backdoor` and `Worms` held out as unknown categories); Held-out
        Test (`345,953` flows) evaluates all four frozen models.
      </p>
      <h2 id="contributions">Current contributions</h2><p>
        The repository now contains the verified data contract, split and
        preprocessing pipelines, Held-out Test evidence for all four models, an
        external cross-dataset audit on `NF-CSE-CIC-IDS2018-v3`, an OOD
        abstention and provenance gate, a localhost FastAPI + SQLite service,
        and a Windows desktop application (`Rust / Tauri 2 + Svelte 5`,
        `v0.6.0`). Live firewall automation and production AES-256-GCM OS
        keyring storage remain deferred (`executed=false` dry-run simulation
        only).
      </p>
      <h2 id="limitations">Limits and next work</h2><p>
        The v3 package was published in 2025, while its observed flows are from
        early 2015. Near-perfect in-domain Validation and Test scores for
        supervised trees fail catastrophically on `NF-CSE-CIC-IDS2018-v3` (RF
        drops to `0.0000` Recall and `0.3802` FPR with `99.99%` `MIN_TTL`
        drift). Meanwhile, Autoencoder reaches only `0.1361` F1 under the strict
        `Val FPR <= 0.05` constraint. Standalone embedded Python packaging and
        full cross-dataset robustness remain future work.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Interpretation rule</strong><br />Unknown-attack holdouts
        (`Backdoor`, `Worms`) test a defined experimental protocol inside the
        2015 testbed; they do not prove zero-day detection in production
        networks.
      </div>
    </article>
    <aside class="source-rail" aria-label="Research sources">
      <span class="eyebrow">Source trail</span><a
        href={source("docs/PROBLEM_STATEMENT.md")}>Problem statement ↗</a
      ><a href={source("docs/METHODOLOGY.md")}>Methodology ↗</a><a
        href={source("docs/MULTI_SOURCE_INGESTION.md")}
        >Multi-source ingestion & OOD gate ↗</a
      ><a
        href={source(
          "reports/evaluation/cross_dataset_generalization_report.md",
        )}>Cross-dataset report ↗</a
      ><a href={source("docs/ROADMAP_FULL_SYSTEM.md")}>Full-system roadmap ↗</a
      ><a href={source("docs/NF_UNSW_NB15_V3_SPLIT_PROTOCOL.md")}
        >Split protocol ↗</a
      ><a href={href("docs/limitations/")}>Read limitations →</a>
    </aside>
  </div>
</Layout>
<style>
  .research-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 250px;
    gap: 5rem;
  }
  .source-rail {
    align-self: start;
    position: sticky;
    top: 110px;
    border-left: 1px solid var(--line);
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.75rem;
  }
  .source-rail a {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  @media (max-width: 800px) {
    .research-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 2rem;
    }
    .source-rail {
      position: static;
    }
  }
</style>
